DATA=[{'filename': 'como-crear-una-zona-de-desayuno-acogedora-en-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Cozy Breakfast Area at Home', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'meta': 'Learn how to create a cozy and practical breakfast area at home with the right location, comfortable furniture, smart storage, lighting, and easy-to-maintain details.', 'intro': 'A well-planned breakfast area can turn an everyday corner into a comfortable place to start the day, even when the kitchen or dining room has limited space.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Choose the Right Location</li>\n<li>Plan the Space Before Buying Furniture</li>\n<li>Choose Comfortable Furniture</li>\n<li>Use Textiles and Lighting to Create Warmth</li>\n<li>Keep the Breakfast Area Organized</li>\n<li>Make Small Spaces Work Harder</li>\n<li>Add Practical Decorative Details</li>\n<li>Avoid Common Breakfast Nook Mistakes</li>\n<li>Adapt the Area to Everyday Life</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A thoughtfully designed breakfast area can transform an underused corner into one of the most pleasant places in the home. It does not need to be large or expensive. A compact table, comfortable seating, suitable lighting, and a few practical details may be enough to create a space for breakfast, coffee, reading, or an informal meal.</p>\n<p>The most important step is to design around the way the household actually lives. A beautiful arrangement that blocks circulation or requires furniture to be moved several times a day quickly becomes inconvenient. Before purchasing anything, observe the room, measure the available area, and decide how many people will normally use the breakfast zone.</p>\n\n<h2>Choose the Right Location</h2>\n<p>Look first for spaces that are already connected to daily routines. A corner near the kitchen, a bright area beside a window, or an unused section of a dining room can work well. Natural light is particularly valuable in the morning because it makes a small area feel more open and inviting.</p>\n<p>However, light should not be the only consideration. Check circulation between the refrigerator, sink, doors, cabinets, and main walkways. Chairs should be able to move without blocking essential paths. If the area is beside a door, make sure seated people will not interfere with opening it.</p>\n<p>Observe the location at the time of day when it will be used most. Strong direct sun can make a seat uncomfortable, while a dark corner may need additional lighting. These simple observations help determine whether the location really suits the intended routine.</p>\n\n<h2>Plan the Space Before Buying Furniture</h2>\n<p>Measure the floor area and mark the approximate dimensions of the table with painter's tape. Then place chairs or temporary objects around the outline to understand how much room is needed for sitting down and standing up. This simple test can prevent buying furniture that looks compact in a showroom but feels oversized at home.</p>\n<p>Consider the number of regular users rather than planning for occasional large gatherings. If two people normally eat breakfast together, a small table for two or three may be more useful than a large table that consumes valuable space every day. Extra guests can often be accommodated elsewhere when necessary.</p>\n<p>Think vertically as well as horizontally. A narrow wall can support shelves for cups, small containers, or decorative objects. Keeping some storage off the floor helps preserve circulation and makes a compact breakfast area feel less crowded.</p>\n\n<h2>Choose Comfortable Furniture</h2>\n<p>The table should match both the room and the way it will be used. Round tables can soften tight corners and remove sharp edges from circulation paths. Rectangular tables may fit better against a wall, while a narrow counter-style surface can work where floor space is especially limited.</p>\n<p>Comfortable seating matters because the area may be used for more than a quick meal. Chairs should provide reasonable support and fit completely beneath or close to the table when not in use. In very small rooms, benches can save space, especially when placed against a wall.</p>\n<p>A built-in or freestanding bench may also provide storage beneath the seat. This can be useful for items that are needed occasionally, but the storage should remain easy to access. Avoid designs that require removing several cushions or moving the entire table whenever something is needed.</p>\n<p>Choose materials that tolerate everyday use. Breakfast areas are exposed to crumbs, drinks, and frequent cleaning. Surfaces that can be wiped easily generally make the space more practical than delicate finishes that require constant care.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Textiles and Lighting to Create Warmth</h2>\n<p>Textiles can make a small breakfast nook feel more comfortable without occupying much space. Seat cushions, a washable runner, or a small rug can introduce texture and color. In an area close to food preparation, washable materials are usually the most practical choice.</p>\n<p>If a rug is used, choose a size that does not create an awkward obstacle when chairs move. The edges should remain flat and stable. In very compact spaces, leaving the floor uncovered may be simpler and easier to clean.</p>\n<p>Lighting should support the morning routine and evening use if necessary. Natural light may be sufficient during the day, while a pendant, wall light, or nearby ceiling fixture can provide additional illumination. The light should reach the tabletop without creating glare at eye level.</p>\n<p>Warmth does not require filling every surface. A few coordinated elements usually create a calmer atmosphere than many small decorations. The goal is to make the breakfast area feel intentional while keeping it easy to clean and use.</p>\n\n<h2>Keep the Breakfast Area Organized</h2>\n<p>Organization is especially important when the breakfast area is small. Keep the tabletop mostly clear so it remains ready for meals. Frequently used items such as napkins or coasters can be stored in a small basket, drawer, or nearby shelf instead of permanently covering the table.</p>\n<p>If the area is close to the kitchen, avoid duplicating storage unnecessarily. Cups, plates, and breakfast foods may already have an efficient place in existing cabinets. The breakfast nook should complement the kitchen rather than becoming another location where miscellaneous objects accumulate.</p>\n<p>A wall shelf can hold a small number of useful or decorative items, but leave visual space between objects. Overloaded shelves can make a compact corner feel smaller and are harder to clean.</p>\n<p>Review the area periodically. Papers, chargers, bags, and other everyday objects often migrate to an unused table. Returning these items to their proper places keeps the breakfast zone available for its intended purpose.</p>\n\n<h2>Make Small Spaces Work Harder</h2>\n<p>In a narrow room, furniture with more than one function can be particularly valuable. A bench with storage, a drop-leaf table, or stools that slide fully underneath a counter can preserve flexibility. Folding solutions are useful only when they are simple enough to operate regularly.</p>\n<p>Use corners carefully. An L-shaped bench can provide several seats without requiring chairs on every side of the table. Before choosing this arrangement, check that people can enter and leave the bench comfortably without forcing everyone else to stand up.</p>\n<p>Mirrors can reflect light and visually open a compact breakfast area, but they should be positioned where reflections are pleasant rather than distracting. A single appropriately sized mirror is often more effective than several small pieces.</p>\n<p>Keep the palette connected to the surrounding room. Repeating a cabinet finish, wood tone, or neutral color can help the breakfast area feel integrated rather than squeezed into leftover space.</p>\n\n<h2>Add Practical Decorative Details</h2>\n<p>Decoration should support the atmosphere without interfering with meals. A small plant, a simple vase, framed artwork, or one attractive tray can be enough. Choose items that are easy to move when the entire tabletop is needed.</p>\n<p>If the area receives good natural light, a suitable houseplant can bring freshness to the corner. Consider mature plant size and watering needs before placing it near upholstered seating or food. A plant that constantly drops leaves or requires frequent moving may not be the best choice.</p>\n<p>Wall art is useful because it adds personality without taking up floor or table space. Hang it at a height that relates visually to the seating area. Oversized pieces can dominate a very small nook, while many tiny frames can make the wall look busy.</p>\n<p>Personal details make the space feel like part of the home. The objective is not to reproduce a showroom image but to create a place that feels comfortable during real breakfasts and ordinary weekdays.</p>\n\n<h2>Avoid Common Breakfast Nook Mistakes</h2>\n<p>One common mistake is choosing furniture before measuring. A table may technically fit while leaving too little room to pull out chairs. Always consider the space required while the furniture is being used, not only its dimensions when everything is pushed into place.</p>\n<p>Another mistake is prioritizing appearance over cleaning. Thick fabrics, delicate surfaces, or complicated decorative arrangements can become frustrating in an area used for food every day. Durable, washable choices usually provide a better long-term result.</p>\n<p>Do not place the area where it blocks essential kitchen circulation. A breakfast nook should make daily life easier, not create congestion around appliances, cabinets, or doors.</p>\n<p>Finally, avoid adding storage simply because there is an empty wall. Storage is useful when it serves a real need. Unnecessary shelves often become places for clutter and make the room feel more crowded.</p>\n\n<h2>Adapt the Area to Everyday Life</h2>\n<p>Use the breakfast area for several weeks before making major additions. Notice which chairs are preferred, whether the table is large enough, and whether the lighting works at different times of day. Real use often reveals small improvements that were impossible to predict during planning.</p>\n<p>If the nook also becomes a place for homework, remote work, or reading, provide a convenient outlet where possible and keep charging cables organized. At the end of the activity, clear work materials so the table can return easily to meal use.</p>\n<p>Seasonal changes may also affect comfort. Morning sunlight shifts during the year, and temperatures near windows can change. Curtains, blinds, cushions, or small furniture adjustments can help the space remain comfortable without redesigning it completely.</p>\n<p>Set a simple maintenance routine. Wipe the table regularly, vacuum or clean beneath seating, wash removable textiles when needed, and inspect furniture hardware occasionally. A well-maintained breakfast area stays inviting with relatively little effort.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How much space is needed for a breakfast area?</h3>\n<p>There is no single minimum size because furniture and circulation needs vary. Measure the location and allow enough room for chairs to be used comfortably without blocking important paths.</p>\n<h3>What type of table works best in a small breakfast nook?</h3>\n<p>Round, compact rectangular, drop-leaf, and narrow counter-style tables can all work. The best option is the one that fits the measured space and normal number of users while preserving circulation.</p>\n<h3>How can a breakfast area feel cozier without adding clutter?</h3>\n<p>Use a limited number of elements such as comfortable cushions, warm lighting, one piece of wall art, or a small plant. Keeping the tabletop mostly clear helps the space remain calm and functional.</p>\n<h3>Can a breakfast nook also be used as a workspace?</h3>\n<p>Yes, provided the table and seating are comfortable enough and work materials can be cleared easily. Good lighting and organized access to power can make occasional work more practical.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Improve Comfort Through Simple Adjustments</h2>\n<p>Comfort often depends on small details rather than additional furniture. Check whether chair height, table position, and the distance to the wall allow people to sit naturally. If a bench feels too deep, a supportive cushion can improve the seating position without changing the entire arrangement.</p>\n<p>Temperature and airflow also influence whether the area is used. A breakfast nook beside a window may feel pleasant in one season and uncomfortable in another. Observe drafts and strong sun, then use blinds, curtains, or small seasonal adjustments when necessary.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Improve Comfort Through Simple Adjustments</h2>\n<p>Comfort often depends on small details rather than additional furniture. Check whether chair height, table position, and the distance to the wall allow people to sit naturally. If a bench feels too deep, a supportive cushion can improve the seating position without changing the entire arrangement.</p>\n<p>Temperature and airflow also influence whether the area is used. A breakfast nook beside a window may feel pleasant in one season and uncomfortable in another. Observe drafts and strong sun, then use blinds, curtains, or small seasonal adjustments when necessary.</p>\n\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A cozy breakfast area is created through proportion, comfort, and practical decisions rather than through size or expense. Choose a location that supports daily routines, measure before buying furniture, preserve circulation, and select surfaces that are easy to maintain. Add lighting, textiles, and decoration with restraint so the space remains useful.</p>\n<p>Start with the essentials and live with the arrangement before adding more. When the breakfast nook responds to the real habits of the household, even a small corner can become a comfortable place to begin the day and enjoy informal moments at home.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-crear-una-zona-de-trabajo-en-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Functional Work Area at Home', 'category': 'HOME ORGANIZATION', 'meta': 'Learn how to create a functional work area at home with the right location, ergonomic furniture, lighting, storage, cable organization, and distraction control.', 'intro': 'Working or studying from home becomes more comfortable when there is a defined place to concentrate, organize materials, and visually separate focused tasks from the rest of domestic life.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Choose the Best Location for the Work Area</li>\n<li>Define What the Space Needs to Support</li>\n<li>Select the Desk and Chair Carefully</li>\n<li>Improve Lighting and Screen Comfort</li>\n<li>Organize Storage and Everyday Materials</li>\n<li>Control Cables and Technology</li>\n<li>Reduce Distractions and Visual Noise</li>\n<li>Make a Small Work Area More Efficient</li>\n<li>Create Better Ergonomic Habits</li>\n<li>Separate Work From the Rest of Home Life</li>\n<li>Maintain the Area Over Time</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Working or studying from home becomes more comfortable when there is a defined place to concentrate, organize materials, and visually separate focused tasks from the rest of domestic life. A good work area does not have to occupy an entire room. A corner of a bedroom, living room, hallway, or dining area can function well when it is planned around light, circulation, storage, ergonomics, and the type of work being done.</p>\n<p>The objective is not to reproduce a perfect office photograph. The best arrangement is the one that fits the available space and daily routine without making the rest of the home harder to use. Before buying furniture, measure carefully, observe noise and light at typical working hours, and decide which tools, documents, and devices must remain close at hand.</p>\n\n<h2>Choose the Best Location for the Work Area</h2>\n<p>Start by observing the home during the hours when the work area will be used most. A place that seems peaceful early in the morning may become a busy passage later. Consider television noise, kitchen activity, conversations, doors, children, pets, and other predictable interruptions before committing to one location.</p>\n<p>Natural light can be useful, but desk position matters. Strong sunlight directly on a monitor can create glare, while sitting with a bright window immediately behind the screen can make the contrast uncomfortable. Test the proposed position at different times of day and use curtains or blinds when necessary.</p>\n<p>Access to power is another practical factor. A laptop, monitor, lamp, phone, or other equipment may need electricity. Avoid creating long extension cords across circulation routes. A slightly less attractive location with safer and simpler power access can be better than a visually perfect corner that creates cable problems.</p>\n<p>Also think about ventilation and temperature. A desk beside a heater, air-conditioning outlet, or strong draft may be uncomfortable during longer sessions. Small environmental details matter more when the same location is used every day.</p>\n\n<h2>Define What the Space Needs to Support</h2>\n<p>Different types of work require different surfaces. A laptop and notebook need less room than a setup with two monitors, documents, drawing tools, or specialized equipment. Make a list of the objects used every day before choosing the desk or storage.</p>\n<p>Separate essential items from occasional ones. Frequently used materials should stay within easy reach, while backup supplies, archived papers, or equipment used only once a month can be stored elsewhere. This prevents the work surface from becoming overloaded.</p>\n<p>Consider whether the area also needs to support studying, video calls, creative work, or household administration. A shared purpose is possible, but each activity should still be practical. For example, a neutral background may be useful for calls, while enough clear desk space may be important for writing or reading.</p>\n\n<h2>Select the Desk and Chair Carefully</h2>\n<p>Measure the available width and depth before shopping. A desk can technically fit against a wall while still leaving too little room for a chair or circulation. Mark the proposed footprint with painter's tape to visualize how the furniture will affect the room.</p>\n<p>The desk should provide enough usable surface without dominating the space. In a small home, a compact desk with nearby vertical storage may work better than a large table that becomes a place for clutter. A shallow desk can be sufficient for laptop-based work if it still allows a comfortable viewing distance.</p>\n<p>The chair is especially important for longer work sessions. It should feel stable, support a comfortable seated position, and allow the user to approach the desk without excessive reaching. Adjustable seating can make adaptation easier, particularly when more than one person uses the area.</p>\n<p>Storage built into the desk can be useful, but not if drawers reduce legroom. Sit at the desk before finalizing the setup and make sure knees, feet, and chair movement are not restricted.</p>\n\n<h2>Improve Lighting and Screen Comfort</h2>\n<p>Use a combination of general room lighting and focused task lighting when possible. A desk lamp can illuminate papers and notebooks without requiring the entire room to be extremely bright. Adjustable lamps are useful because the direction can change depending on the activity.</p>\n<p>For computer work, position the monitor to minimize glare and strong reflections. You may need to adjust the desk angle, screen position, lamp, or blinds. Avoid working for long periods with a bright screen in a completely dark room because the contrast can become uncomfortable.</p>\n<p>If handwriting is common, place the task light so the writing hand does not cast a large shadow across the page. The ideal side depends on whether the user is right- or left-handed.</p>\n<p>Keep lamps secure and cables away from feet or chair wheels. A tidy lighting setup is not only more attractive but also safer and easier to clean.</p>\n\n<h2>Organize Storage and Everyday Materials</h2>\n<p>Keep the items used every day close to the desk. Pens, notebooks, chargers, frequently consulted documents, and current project materials should have defined places that are easy to access and easy to reset after work.</p>\n<p>Use vertical storage when floor space is limited. Wall shelves, pegboards, or narrow bookcases can add capacity without enlarging the desk. Any wall-mounted solution should be securely installed and positioned so objects cannot fall onto the user or equipment.</p>\n<p>A simple category system is usually easier to maintain than many tiny compartments. For example, one area for writing tools, one tray for active documents, and one shelf for reference materials may be enough.</p>\n<p>Paper tends to accumulate quickly. Set a regular time to discard unnecessary drafts, file important documents, and move completed work out of the active zone. The goal is to preserve enough clear desk surface for the next task.</p>\n\n<h2>Control Cables and Technology</h2>\n<p>Cables can make even an organized work area look chaotic. Group them where possible, keep excess length off the floor, and separate power cables from walking routes. Cable clips, sleeves, or a simple tray beneath the desk can help if they are used without restricting ventilation.</p>\n<p>Do not overload outlets or power strips. Equipment should remain accessible enough to disconnect safely when necessary. Avoid hiding adapters beneath fabrics, inside tightly closed boxes, or in other places where heat can accumulate.</p>\n<p>If devices are charged frequently, create one predictable charging location. This reduces loose cables across the desk and makes it easier to leave the surface clear when work ends.</p>\n<p>Review technology periodically. Old chargers, unused cables, and accessories can remain for months simply because they are small. Removing what is no longer needed improves both organization and safety.</p>\n\n<h2>Reduce Distractions and Visual Noise</h2>\n<p>A work area should visually support concentration. This does not mean it has to be empty, but unrelated objects should be limited. When every shelf contains decoration, paperwork, and household items, the brain has more visual information to process.</p>\n<p>Digital distractions require habits as much as furniture. Notifications can be silenced during focused periods, and entertainment devices can remain out of immediate reach when they are not needed for the task.</p>\n<p>If the work area shares a room with the television or another busy activity, changing the direction of the desk may help. Facing a calmer wall can reduce visual movement, while a small screen or open shelf may create a sense of separation without building a permanent partition.</p>\n<p>Decoration can still be personal. One plant suited to the light, a calendar, a small piece of art, or a meaningful object can make the area pleasant without filling every available surface.</p>\n\n<h2>Make a Small Work Area More Efficient</h2>\n<p>In compact homes, furniture should correspond precisely to the work being done. A wall-mounted fold-down desk can be useful when the area must disappear after working hours, while a narrow desk may fit into a bedroom or hallway without blocking circulation.</p>\n<p>Mobile storage can add flexibility. A small rolling cart can hold work supplies during the day and move elsewhere afterward. This is particularly useful when the table is shared with dining or another household activity.</p>\n<p>Use height strategically. A shelf above the desk can hold reference material or supplies, but keep heavy items at safe heights and leave enough headroom. The wall should support the storage rather than becoming overloaded.</p>\n<p>Keep the floor as clear as possible. Storage boxes, cables, and bags around chair legs make the area harder to clean and reduce freedom of movement. A compact workspace feels larger when the floor remains visually open.</p>\n\n<h2>Create Better Ergonomic Habits</h2>\n<p>Comfort depends on both furniture and behavior. Sit with the body supported and avoid leaning forward for long periods. Feet should have stable support, and the shoulders should remain reasonably relaxed while typing or writing.</p>\n<p>For screen work, place the monitor at a comfortable viewing distance and adjust height or angle as needed. Laptop users who work for extended periods may benefit from an external keyboard or another setup that allows the screen and hands to be positioned more independently.</p>\n<p>No single posture should be maintained for hours. Stand up regularly, move around, and give the eyes short breaks from close screen work. The work area should make movement easy rather than encouraging complete immobility.</p>\n<p>If several people use the same workstation, adjustable elements are especially helpful. The chair, monitor, and accessories may need to change between users.</p>\n\n<h2>Separate Work From the Rest of Home Life</h2>\n<p>One advantage of a defined work area is the ability to create a clear beginning and end to the workday. Start by turning on the task light, opening the necessary materials, and preparing the desk. At the end, close documents, put away supplies, and clear the main surface.</p>\n<p>This routine is particularly useful when the desk is located in a bedroom or living room. Work materials should not spread across the entire space and remain visible at all times. A drawer, cabinet, basket, or portable file box can help the room return to its normal function after work.</p>\n<p>If possible, keep meals and leisure activities away from the desk. The physical distinction between work and rest can make it easier to concentrate when working and disconnect afterward.</p>\n<p>When the same surface must serve more than one purpose, create a fast reset system. A tray or portable organizer can hold active work materials and be moved away when the table needs to be used for something else.</p>\n\n<h2>Maintain the Area Over Time</h2>\n<p>Spend a few minutes at the end of each work session returning supplies to their places and removing cups, papers, and unrelated objects. A small daily reset prevents the work area from turning into general household storage.</p>\n<p>Once a week, review papers, wipe surfaces, clean equipment according to manufacturer guidance, and check whether cables or storage are becoming messy. Short maintenance sessions are easier than waiting until the entire area requires reorganization.</p>\n<p>Observe your habits. If you repeatedly avoid a drawer, move the lamp, or place documents on the floor, the original organization may not match real use. Adjust the system instead of forcing an inconvenient routine.</p>\n<p>Needs change over time. New projects may require more reference material, a second monitor, or different tools. Keep enough flexibility to adapt without replacing every piece of furniture.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Where is the best place for a work area at home?</h3>\n<p>The best location is one with manageable noise, adequate light, safe power access when needed, and enough room for a desk and chair without blocking circulation.</p>\n<h3>Does a home work area need a large desk?</h3>\n<p>No. The desk should be large enough for the tasks performed there. A compact surface with good organization can be more effective than oversized furniture.</p>\n<h3>How can I work in a shared room?</h3>\n<p>Choose quieter periods, orient the desk away from distractions, keep unrelated objects off the work surface, and agree on reasonable quiet conditions with other household members when possible.</p>\n<h3>How can I stop the desk from becoming cluttered?</h3>\n<p>Keep only current work materials on the surface, create defined storage for supplies and papers, and reset the desk briefly at the end of each session.</p>\n\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A functional work area at home is built around concentration, comfort, and organization. Choose the location according to real household routines, provide suitable lighting and seating, keep essential materials accessible, and manage cables and distractions carefully. The space does not have to be large to work well.</p>\n<p>Start with the essentials, observe how the area is actually used, and adjust the layout as needs change. When furniture, storage, technology, and everyday habits support one another, even a small corner can become a reliable place for focused work or study while the rest of the home remains organized.</p>\n"}]

def wc(h):
    return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b", re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", h)))

print("="*68)
print("NEXT BATCH - ARTICLES 27 AND 28 - CORRECTED")
